=== A General Exhortation

#smallcaps[Having] now gone through the considerations of the duty of watching so that we do not enter into temptation, I suppose I need not add motives for keeping it. Those who are not moved by their own sad experiences, or by the importance of the duty as laid down at the beginning of this discourse, must be left by me to the further patience of God. I will only close the whole with a general exhortation to those who are in any measure prepared for it by considering what has been said. Suppose you#super[+] went into a hospital and saw many persons lying sick and weak, sore and wounded, with many foul diseases and disorders. Suppose you#super[+] asked them how they fell into this condition, and they all agreed in telling you#super[+] that this or that thing was the occasion of it: “That is how I got my wound,” says one; “And my disease,” says another. Would it not make you#super[+] a little careful how you#super[+] dealt with that thing or place, and what you#super[+] had to do with it? Surely it would. Suppose you#super[+] went to a dungeon and saw many miserable creatures bound in chains for a coming day of execution. Suppose you#super[+] asked by what way and means they were brought into that condition, and they all fixed on one and the same thing. Would you#super[+] not take care to avoid it? So it is with entering into temptation. Ah! how many poor, miserable, spiritually wounded souls we have everywhere! One is wounded by one sin, another by another; one falls into filthiness of the flesh, another into filthiness of the spirit. Ask them now how they came into this state and condition. They must all answer, “Alas! we entered into temptation; we fell into cursed snares and entanglements; and that has brought us into the woeful condition you see!” Indeed, if a man could look into the dungeons of hell and see the poor damned souls who lie bound in chains of darkness, and hear their cries, what would he be taught? What do they say? Are they not cursing their tempters, and the temptations they entered into? And shall we be negligent in this? Solomon tells us that the simple man who follows the strange woman “does not know that the dead are there,” that “her house sinks down to death, and her tracks to the departed spirits” (and he repeats this three times); and that is why he ventures on her snares. If you#super[+] knew what has been done by entering into temptation, perhaps you#super[+] would be more watchful and careful. Men may think they will do well enough in spite of it. But “Can a man embrace fire and his clothes not be burned? Can a man walk on hot coals without scorching his feet?” (Prov 6:27, 28). No such thing; men do not come out of their temptation without wounds, burns, and scars. I do not know any place in the world where there is more need to press this exhortation than in this place. Go to our various colleges and ask after this or that young man. What is the answer in the case of many? “Ah! that one was very promising for a season, but he fell into bad company, and he is quite lost. That one had some good beginnings of religion, and we had great expectations of him; but he has fallen into temptation.” And so it is in other places. “That one was useful and humble, and adorned the gospel; but now he is so woefully entangled with the world that he has become all self, and has no sap or savor. That one was humble and zealous; but he has been promoted, and has lost his first love and ways.” Oh! how full the world is, how full this place is, of these woeful examples, to say nothing of the countless poor creatures who have fallen into temptation through delusions in religion! And is it not time for us to wake up before it is too late, to watch against the first risings of sin, the first attempts of Satan, and all the ways by which he has made his approaches to us, however harmless they may be in themselves?

Do we not have experience of our weakness, our folly, and #emph[the invincible power] of temptation once it has got within us? As for this duty I have dwelt on, take these considerations:

1\. If you#super[+] neglect it, since it is the only means prescribed by our Savior, you#super[+] will certainly enter into temptation, and just as certainly fall into sin. Do not flatter yourselves. Some of you#super[+] are “old disciples” and have a great abhorrence of sin. You#super[+] think it impossible that you#super[+] should ever be seduced in this way or that. But “the one (whoever he is) who thinks he is standing firm should be careful not to fall.” It is not any grace received, not any experience gained, not any resolve made use of, that will preserve you#super[+] from any evil, unless you#super[+] stand on your#super[+] watch. “What I say to you#super[+],” says Christ, “I say to everyone: Keep watch!” Perhaps you#super[+] have had some good success for a time in your#super[+] careless frame. But wake up, and wonder at God’s tenderness and patience, or evil lies at the door. If you#super[+] will not perform this duty, whoever you#super[+] are, you#super[+] will be tempted and defiled one way or another, in one thing or another, in spiritual or fleshly wickedness. And what will be the end of it? Remember Peter!

2\. Consider that you#super[+] are always under the eye of Christ, the great captain of our salvation, who has commanded us to watch in this way and to pray that we will not enter into temptation. What do you#super[+] think are the thoughts and the heart of Christ when he sees a temptation hurrying toward us, a storm rising around us, and we are fast asleep? Does it not grieve him to see us expose ourselves to danger like this, after he has given us warning upon warning? While he was in the days of his flesh, he considered his temptation while it was still coming, and armed himself against it. “The prince of this world is coming,” he says, but “he has nothing in Me.” And shall we be negligent under his eye? Only think that you see him coming to you as he came to Peter when he was asleep in the garden, with the same rebuke: “Were you not able to keep watch for one hour?” Would it not grieve you to be rebuked like that, or to hear him thundering against your neglect from heaven, as he did against the church of Sardis (Rev 3:2)?

3\. Consider this. If you neglect this duty, and so fall into temptation, as you certainly will, then when you are entangled God may also bring some heavy affliction or judgment on you. Because of your entanglement you will not be able to see it as anything other than evidence of his anger and hatred. And then what will you do with your temptation and your affliction together? All your bones will be broken, and your peace and strength will be gone in a moment. This may seem only a noise of words for now; but if it is ever your condition, you will find it full of woe and bitterness. Oh, then, let us strive to keep our spirits unentangled, avoiding every appearance of evil and all ways that lead to it; especially all ways, business, company, and employments that we have already found harmful to us.
